WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.890 --> 00:00:02.504
A part comes off the machine.

2
00:00:03.194 --> 00:00:07.571
Whether it is good, you only know when you measure it and can trust that measuring.

3
00:00:08.545 --> 00:00:12.644
First the surface, the gauges, and what heat does to a size.

4
00:00:13.713 --> 00:00:16.964
Then: how much tolerance does the measuring itself use up?

5
00:00:17.737 --> 00:00:20.210
And how does the process stay capable all day?

6
00:00:21.523 --> 00:00:24.461
A roughness tester draws a fine stylus across the

7
00:00:24.507 --> 00:00:27.839
surface and traces its tiny peaks and valleys.

8
00:00:28.548 --> 00:00:32.589
Ra is their average height, in microns: the lower, the smoother.

9
00:00:33.332 --> 00:00:40.194
Rough turning gives about 3.2 to 6.3, fine turning 0.8 to 1.6.

10
00:00:41.185 --> 00:00:46.154
A regular wavy pattern is chatter: vibration, because the tool sticks out too far,

11
00:00:46.340 --> 00:00:48.348
the wall is thin or the speed is wrong.

12
00:00:49.370 --> 00:00:54.549
Where the tool runs out, burrs are left: sharp ridges, often from worn tools.

13
00:00:55.861 --> 00:01:00.563
In practice a caliper is good to about two or three hundredths of a millimetre.

14
00:01:01.304 --> 00:01:04.497
A micrometer, a measuring clamp with a fine screw,

15
00:01:04.694 --> 00:01:08.235
reads one micron: enough for plus or minus one hundredth.

16
00:01:09.193 --> 00:01:12.990
The rule of thumb: read about ten times finer than the tolerance.

17
00:01:13.711 --> 00:01:17.182
And calibrate on a schedule, against a known reference.

18
00:01:18.254 --> 00:01:23.735
For holes there is the go/no-go plug gauge: one end must fit, the other must not.

19
00:01:24.525 --> 00:01:28.031
No number to read, so it is quick and hard to get wrong.

20
00:01:29.417 --> 00:01:32.145
For form and position you go to the measuring room.

21
00:01:32.841 --> 00:01:37.565
A CMM, a coordinate measuring machine, touches points in the bore with a probe,

22
00:01:37.752 --> 00:01:39.668
a small pin with a ball on its tip.

23
00:01:40.693 --> 00:01:44.164
From them it works out diameter, roundness and position,

24
00:01:44.280 --> 00:01:45.929
from the datums on the drawing.

25
00:01:46.922 --> 00:01:52.495
The room is kept at 20 degrees: the reference temperature for sizes, from ISO 1.

26
00:01:53.803 --> 00:01:58.854
Steel expands as it warms up, about eleven and a half microns per metre per degree.

27
00:01:59.539 --> 00:02:01.014
Aluminium twice that.

28
00:02:02.053 --> 00:02:08.334
A shaft of thirty, h6, must measure between 29.987 and thirty.

29
00:02:09.015 --> 00:02:15.749
The operator measures it warm from the machine, at 35 degrees: 29.990.

30
00:02:16.445 --> 00:02:16.712
Good.

31
00:02:17.737 --> 00:02:20.314
But the customer measures at twenty degrees.

32
00:02:21.075 --> 00:02:27.449
There the shaft has shrunk by five microns, to 29.985: out of tolerance.

33
00:02:28.160 --> 00:02:29.507
The batch is rejected.

34
00:02:30.522 --> 00:02:34.435
So: let the part cool for a quarter of an hour in a set place,

35
00:02:34.644 --> 00:02:37.964
or set the gauge with a master part at the same temperature.

36
00:02:39.317 --> 00:02:41.790
Every measurement has a measurement uncertainty:

37
00:02:42.033 --> 00:02:45.458
a range around the reading in which the true value lies.

38
00:02:46.161 --> 00:02:50.434
From the instrument, the person, the part, the temperature and the method.

39
00:02:51.580 --> 00:02:54.529
The rule of ten times finer is about resolution:

40
00:02:54.691 --> 00:02:57.106
the smallest step the instrument shows.

41
00:02:58.120 --> 00:03:01.580
At plus or minus two hundredths the band is four hundredths,

42
00:03:01.696 --> 00:03:04.273
so that step is four microns at most.

43
00:03:05.306 --> 00:03:08.951
But a gauge can show one micron and still scatter by five.

44
00:03:10.255 --> 00:03:13.529
So you test the whole measuring system with a gauge R&amp;R:

45
00:03:13.645 --> 00:03:16.315
repeatability and reproducibility together,

46
00:03:16.489 --> 00:03:18.590
the random spread of the measuring system.

47
00:03:19.296 --> 00:03:23.917
For example ten parts, three operators, each part three times,

48
00:03:24.091 --> 00:03:25.681
blind and in random order.

49
00:03:26.707 --> 00:03:31.618
Repeatability is the spread when one person measures the same part again and again:

50
00:03:31.758 --> 00:03:33.221
mostly the instrument.

51
00:03:34.289 --> 00:03:38.352
Reproducibility is the difference between people: method and handling.

52
00:03:39.411 --> 00:03:42.128
On a shaft of plus or minus two hundredths,

53
00:03:42.337 --> 00:03:46.284
the standard deviation of the measuring is one and a half microns.

54
00:03:47.017 --> 00:03:51.696
Six times that is nine microns: 22.5 percent of the band.

55
00:03:52.465 --> 00:03:55.298
By the AIAG manual, under ten percent is good,

56
00:03:55.472 --> 00:03:58.583
up to thirty conditionally acceptable, above that not.

57
00:03:59.355 --> 00:04:01.433
The SPC module goes deeper.

58
00:04:02.780 --> 00:04:05.206
The machine grows too: thermal growth.

59
00:04:05.889 --> 00:04:10.661
Spindle, ball screws and frame expand, and positions shift by hundredths,

60
00:04:10.800 --> 00:04:12.275
mostly while warming up.

61
00:04:13.312 --> 00:04:16.516
Cpk is the distance from the mean to the nearest limit,

62
00:04:16.714 --> 00:04:19.849
divided by three sigma: does it fit where it is?

63
00:04:20.547 --> 00:04:22.463
The quality module explains it.

64
00:04:23.517 --> 00:04:29.833
A bore of 25, plus or minus fifteen microns, a standard deviation of three microns.

65
00:04:30.552 --> 00:04:35.034
In the morning the average sits on size: Cpk 1.67.

66
00:04:35.764 --> 00:04:39.143
Many customers ask 1.33 in series.

67
00:04:40.160 --> 00:04:45.814
By the afternoon heat and wear have moved the average to 25.006:

68
00:04:46.000 --> 00:04:47.950
nine microns left to the limit,

69
00:04:48.147 --> 00:04:49.122
Cpk 1.0.

70
00:04:49.840 --> 00:04:52.371
The same spread; only the average walked.

71
00:04:53.714 --> 00:04:56.547
So the fix is not straight away a new machine.

72
00:04:57.258 --> 00:05:01.739
A warm-up cycle runs the spindle and axes at stepped speeds first,

73
00:05:01.879 --> 00:05:04.282
about twenty minutes on some machines.

74
00:05:05.294 --> 00:05:07.454
And in-machine probing closes the loop:

75
00:05:07.593 --> 00:05:10.031
a touch probe in the spindle measures the bore.

76
00:05:11.037 --> 00:05:13.196
The control corrects the wear offset,

77
00:05:13.370 --> 00:05:19.315
and the average stays within one micron of size: Cpk 1.56, all day.

78
00:05:19.994 --> 00:05:22.629
Provided the probe itself is calibrated.

79
00:05:23.674 --> 00:05:27.251
So: get the measuring right first, then judge the process.
